247:
Food trucks shall locate on privately owned lots. Operation within the cultural heritage tourism zone is prohibited except for special events as approved by the city. Food trucks will be located on all-weather surface (or gravel) parking areas accessible by emergency vehicles. and having at least ten legally designated off-street parking spaces, subject to the approval of the property owner and subject to the following:
(1) Written notice to the McIntosh County Health Department 14 days prior to operations begin. Such notice shall state the expected dates of operation and shall specify the physical location of operations.
(2) The food truck shall be positioned at least 50 feet from the customer entrance of an existing restaurant during the food truck's hours of operation;
(3) No food truck shall be operated within 600 feet of any public school in the city between the hours of 7:30 a.m. and 3:30 p.m. on days in which schools are actually in session;
(4) The food truck shall be positioned on designated parking spaces and shall not block drive aisles, other access to loading/service areas, or emergency access and fire lanes;
(5) No food truck shall be located within a building setback or landscape buffer;
(6) The food truck must be positioned at least 15 feet away from fire hydrants, driveway entrances, alleys, handicapped parking spaces, tree trunks, and vegetation;
(7) Food trucks shall not be located within 100 feet of gas pumps, underground gas storage tanks, and vent pipes;
(8) Food trucks may not be located in any portion of a parking lot when and where such location would prevent the use of parking spaces during the regular hours of operation of the primary business on the lot, or otherwise interfere in a significant [manner] with the movement of motor vehicles, unless prior permission to use the parking spots has been provided by the property owner;
(9) Cooking off the vehicle with a grill or smoker is prohibited;
(10) No seating shall be provided, although high top tables are allowed but shall not exceed three high top tables total;
(11) Vehicle-mounted roll-out awnings are allowed; and
(12) Vehicle must vacate premises by 10:00 p.m. and return to base of operation daily.
(Ord. No. 2022-14, § I(Exh. A), 12-20-2022)

270:
The city manager shall select and appoint on behalf of the city a qualified attorney to serve as solicitor in the municipal court of the city. The duties of the solicitor are:
(1) To attend each session of the municipal court unless excused by the judge thereof and to remain until the business of the city is disposed of;
(2) To aid the municipal court judge in organizing the court as he may require;
(3) To draw up all indictments or accusations, when necessary;
(4) To attend before the appellate courts when any ordinance violation case emanating from the municipal court of the city is tried, to argue the same, and to perform any other duty therein which the interest of the city may require;
(5) To advise law enforcement and code enforcement officers concerning the sufficiency of evidence, warrants, and similar matters relating to the investigation and prosecution of Code violations;
(6) To represent the city prosecuting all charges brought or initiated by any city employee acting in the course of his or her employment with the city;
(7) To perform such other duties as are or may be required by law or which necessarily appertain to their office.
The appointment shall be made for a term consisting of one calendar year or portion thereof. The compensation and other terms of employment shall be set forth in a contract between the city and the solicitor.
(Ord. of 4-17-2008(2), § 26-10)

290:
(a) Appointment. The following election officials shall be appointed by the mayor and council and shall receive such compensation as is provided by the same:
(1) Municipal election superintendent;
(2) Chief manager;
(3) Two assistant managers;
(4) Clerks, as necessary.
